  "change": "release.yml fires a second cron on its one `schedule:` (`17 1 * * *`, beside the 6-hourly `0 */6 * * *`); the two clocks are told apart by `github.event.schedule`, as fleet-patrols.yml does. The daily cron starts `snapshot-next-preflight` alone (the unchanged dispatch preflight; on a schedule the summary says the clock started it and that a refusal is the expected daily red until `@objectstack/service-push` debuts); on a pass `snapshot-next` waits at `environment: release` as before. `version-pr` now names its own cron (strictly subtractive); every stable job plus `stale-prompts` is skipped on the daily cron. New job `snapshot-next-supersede` (needs the preflight, `success() && <daily cron>`, `actions: write`) runs `snapshot-next.mjs supersede`, which cancels only an OLDER `schedule` run whose snapshot job is still `waiting` at `release` — never a dispatch, a started job, a stable prompt or the calling run; an unanswered read defers to tomorrow (exit 0, warning). `cancel-in-progress` stays false. ADR-0125 gains `## Amendment (2026-11-06, #30832)` in the house shape plus dated pointer callouts at D1/D4/D5 and the three replaced 2026-11-05 sentences; D1-D6 and earlier amendments untouched. docs/releases-maintenance.md row: proposed daily by schedule, published on approval (own commit). Pins: snapshot-next.mjs `declaredCrons`/`guardClock`, clock + supersede pins in `snapshotWiring`, three new batteries (floor 13→16, 123 cases/16 batteries); fleet-write `jobsStartedBy` gains a `schedule` argument and its release.yml pins evaluate each cron (340 cases/17 batteries). Draft PR, `skip-changeset`.",
